# Per-Agent Artifacts Brain Repos

## Decision

Each long-lived campaign mind gets its **own Cloudflare Artifacts repo**.

Do not put all player Brain notes into one shared campaign repo.

Canonical split:

```txt
artifact repo: agent-dungeon-campaign-{campaignId}-player-a
  private PlayerAgent A Brain only

artifact repo: agent-dungeon-campaign-{campaignId}-player-b
  private PlayerAgent B Brain only

artifact repo: agent-dungeon-campaign-{campaignId}-referee
  private Referee / RefereeAgent Brain only

artifact repo: agent-dungeon-campaign-{campaignId}-table
  revealed shared table artifacts only
```

## Why

Fog-of-war is a capability firewall, not just a prompt instruction.

Per-agent repos make the privacy boundary structural:

- `PlayerAgent[player-a]` cannot casually read `player-b` notes.
- Player private goals, fears, theories, grudges, and plans stay in that player's repo.
- Referee hidden fronts, NPC secrets, and raw/near-raw rule research stay in the Referee repo.
- The table repo contains only revealed artifacts: party log, shared rumors, maps, visible inventory, public handouts.
- Repo-scoped Artifacts tokens naturally match least-privilege access.
- Repos can be inspected, forked, diffed, archived, or revoked independently.

This matches Cloudflare Artifacts' intended grain better than one shared blob: one repo per agent/user/task, repo-scoped tokens, and versioned file trees that can be forked or inspected through Git.

## Access model

```txt
PlayerAgent[player-a]
  read/write: player-a repo
  read: table repo
  no access: player-b repo, referee repo, raw OSE corpus

PlayerAgent[player-b]
  read/write: player-b repo
  read: table repo
  no access: player-a repo, referee repo, raw OSE corpus

Referee / RefereeAgent
  read/write: referee repo
  read/write: table repo
  may read player public committed intents/events
  should not read player-private repos by default

Monitor
  read: table projection only
  optional dev mode: Referee audit projection, never player-private repos
```

## File shape

Player repo:

```txt
/profile.md
/current.md
/goals.md
/fears.md
/theories.md
/relationships.md
/skills/player-survival.md
/skills/class-thief.md
/memories/beat-0003.md
```

Referee repo:

```txt
/fronts.md
/npcs/hesta-vane.md
/rules/receipts.md
/skills/referee-role.md
/skills/reaction-checks.md
/hidden-threads.md
```

Table repo:

```txt
/party-log.md
/revealed-rumors.md
/shared-map-notes.md
/visible-inventory.md
/handouts/...
```

## Implementation notes

Start hot and simple:

1. Keep latest compact summaries in Agent state so beats do not need Git round trips.
2. After each beat, append/update compressed Brain notes.
3. Sync those notes to the appropriate Artifacts repo.
4. Use Git commits/git notes for durable provenance: beat id, agent id, model, rule receipt ids, and summary.

Do not store raw full OSE chunks in player repos or the table repo. Referee can store doc/chunk IDs and short private summaries in its own repo.
